{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791458063,"nonce":"TFchMPlaXXA61TbUCGSXwNtmDr8=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABV13nRw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/158","origin":"github"},"title":"Hoist numeric literals so rebuilding a form with different constants reuses the compiled program","message":"## Problem\n\nChanging a plain number in a term list forces a rebuild and a fresh XLA compile, even when the structure of the problem is identical. Continuation walks change several coefficients many times in a row (a viscosity cap, a Perzyna rate, a DEVSS weight, a blend factor). Written as constants, every step recompiles. On a 3-D rolling model, earlier measurements put about 6 s of each ~7.6 s solve into recompiling.\n\nToday the workaround is to declare each such coefficient with `jno.np.parameter((1,), name=...)` and pass its value to `fem.solve(name=value)` or a `continuation=` sequence. That works, but it puts the burden on the user: they have to know which constants will change, use a \"trainable parameter\" for something that is never trained, and thread every value through `.solve`.\n\n## Proposed solution\n\nHoist float literals internally (\"literal hoisting\"), with no new user-facing API. When a term list is traced, numeric literals become inputs of the compiled program instead of baked-in constants. The compile cache is then keyed on the structure of the form, ignoring literal values, so rebuilding the same form with different numbers reuses the executable.\n\n```python\n# user writes plain constants; the second build reuses the first one's executable\nfor eta in (0.03, 0.01, 0.003, 0.001):\n    fem = jno.fem(terms(eta_p=eta))\n    x = fem.solve(x0=x)\n```\n\nWhat has to hold:\n1. **Structural constants stay structural.** A coefficient that changes term classification (a zero that removes a term, a value that turns a nonlinear form linear) or a Python-level branch on a value must still produce a different program. Only values that leave the classification unchanged are hoisted.\n2. **Constant folding.** XLA simplifies `0*x`, `x**2.0` and similar when the number is known. Hoisting every literal may slow kernels slightly, which needs measuring on a real form (e.g. a Taylor–Hood flow problem and an H(curl) problem).\n3. **Cache key.** It ignores literal values but keeps everything else (mesh, spaces, term structure, solver slots). This extends the existing compiled-solve cache rather than adding a second mechanism.\n4. **Differentiability unchanged.** A hoisted literal is an input, so gradients with respect to it could even come for free; `jno.np.parameter` stays the explicit way to declare something trainable.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **`jno.np.parameter` + `fem.solve(name=value)`.** Works today, but leaks performance concerns into the problem statement and forces a trainable placeholder for a fixed number.\n- **`jno.solve.continuation(...)`.** Cleaner for a ramp, but still requires declaring the parameters, and does not help when a user simply rebuilds with a different constant.\n- **Hoist only literals marked by the user** (e.g. a `jno.np.const(...)` wrapper). Less magic, but it is the same burden under another name.\n\n## Additional context\n\nFirst step before building: a probe that rebuilds one form at two coefficient values and checks that (a) the recompile disappears, (b) the solution is bit-identical to the baked-constant build, and (c) solve time is unchanged within noise.","files":null}]}